An optional top row, off by default.

** Why it is off by default
A header line is not the row above the mode line. Window geometry, top to
bottom, is: header line, text area, mode line, echo area. A header row
therefore sits at the *top* of the window with the whole buffer between it
and the bar — it is not a second line of the same strip, and it costs a row
in every window whether or not you are looking at it.

The stacked rows this build is for live in `mode-line-format`, where a "\n"
really does put one line above the other. The header is here because an
cyberdeck-context row at the top is a genuinely different thing and some people
want it, not because the bar needs it.

It is also off by default because `olivetti-mode' — enabled in the dashboard
at MM_ORDER 86, after this — takes over `header-line-format` when it runs.
Turning this on and using olivetti outside the dashboard is a conflict, so
the toggle is explicit.

** Toggle
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defvar manifolding-modeline-header-p nil
  "Non-nil to show the optional top row.
Off by default: see the AIU above on window geometry and on olivetti.")

(defun manifolding-modeline-header-format ()
  "The header construct: keyboard state, then AIU Frame context.
The state name is the same function the bar uses, so the two rows can
never report different things."
   (when manifolding-modeline-header-p
     (let ((state (and (fboundp 'biomechanical-input-interface-modeline-state-name)
                       (biomechanical-input-interface-modeline-state-name)))
           (aiu-frame (and (boundp 'manifolding-modeline--cyberdeck-cache)
                       manifolding-modeline--cyberdeck-cache)))
       (when (or state aiu-frame)
         (append (when state (list (propertize state 'face 'mode-line)))
                 (when (and state aiu-frame) (list "  "))
                 (when aiu-frame
                   (list (propertize aiu-frame 'face 'manifolding-modeline-info-face))))))))

(defun manifolding-modeline-toggle-header ()
  "Show or hide the optional top row."
  (interactive)
  (setq manifolding-modeline-header-p (not manifolding-modeline-header-p))
  (setq header-line-format (manifolding-modeline-header-format))
  (force-mode-line-update t)
  (message "Cyberdeck modeline header %s"
           (if manifolding-modeline-header-p "on" "off")))
#+end_src

** Manual extract — Window Header Lines
Migrated from `the-screen/display/06-screen'.

A window can have a header line at the top, just as it can have a mode line
at the bottom. The header line feature works just like the mode line
feature, except that it's controlled by header-line-format.

header-line-format
This variable, local in every buffer, specifies how to display the header
line, for windows displaying the buffer. The format of the value is the same
as for mode-line-format. It is normally nil, so that ordinary buffers have
no header line.

If display-line-numbers-mode is turned on in a buffer, the buffer text is
indented on display by the amount of screen space needed to show the line
numbers. By contrast, text of the header line is not automatically indented,
because a header line never displays a line number, and because the text of
the header line is not necessarily directly related to buffer text below it.
If a Lisp program needs the header-line text to be aligned with buffer text
(for example, if the buffer displays columnar data, like tabulated-list-mode
does), it should turn on the minor mode header-line-indent-mode.

header-line-indent-mode
This buffer-local minor mode tracks the changes of the width of the
line-number display on screen (which may vary depending on the range of line
numbers shown in the window), and allows Lisp programs to arrange that
header-line text is always aligned with buffer text when the line-number
width changes. Such Lisp programs should turn on this mode in the buffer, and
use the variables header-line-indent and header-line-indent-width in the
header-line-format to ensure it is adjusted to the text indentation at all
times.

header-line-indent
This variable's value is a whitespace string whose width is kept equal to
the current width of line-numbers on display, provided that
header-line-indent-mode is turned on in the buffer shown in the window. The
number of spaces is calculated under the assumption that the face of the
header-line text uses the same font, including size, as the frame's default
font; if that assumption is false, use header-line-indent-width, described
below, instead. This variable is intended to be used in simple situations
where the header-line text needs to be indented as a whole to be realigned
with buffer text, by prepending this variable's value to the actual
header-line text. For example, the following definition of
header-line-format:

  (setq header-line-format
        `("" header-line-indent ,my-header-line))

where my-header-line is the format string that produces the actual text of
the header line, will make sure the header-line text is always indented like
the buffer text below it.

header-line-indent-width
This variable's value is kept updated to provide the current width, in units
of the frame's canonical character width, used for displaying the line
numbers, provided that header-line-indent-mode is turned on in the buffer
shown in the window. It can be used for aligning the header-line text with
the buffer text when header-line-indent is not flexible enough. For example,
if the header line uses a font whose metrics is different from the default
face's font, your Lisp program can calculate the width of line-number
display in pixels, by multiplying the value of this variable by the value
returned by frame-char-width, and then use the result to align the header
line text using the :align-to display property spec in pixels on the relevant
parts of header-line-format.

window-header-line-height &optional window
This function returns the height in pixels of window's header line. window
must be a live window, and defaults to the selected window.

A window that is just one line tall never displays a header line. A window
that is two lines tall cannot display both a mode line and a header line at
once; if it has a mode line, then it does not display a header line.

That last constraint is the other reason the header is off by default: on a
phone, windows are short, and a two-line window that would show both is
exactly the kind of window a phone gives you.
